$ todoer help
todoer - todos and pull requests of a git project as plain files, for people and AI agents

A todo is a file .todoer/todos/<name>.md. The name is its id and its title.
A pull request is a folder .todoer/prs/<name>/ with a patch and a review, on the branch that it
goes into. The name is its id and its title.
todoer reads and writes the files of the current checkout, on any branch. todoer never commits:
you commit the changes.

If you are an AI agent, run 'todoer skill' to learn the todoer workflow.

Todos:
  ls [--json]               list the todos and their open pull requests; outside a git
                            repository, list the todos of each project in the current folder
                            and its subfolders, with its path and its checked-out branch
  show <name> [--json]      print the file, the pull requests and the text of a todo
  add <name> [text]         add a todo; the text can come on stdin
  done <name>...            remove the todos

Pull requests:
  pr submit --branch b -n name [-m text] [--update]
                            run it on the branch that the pull request goes into; squash the
                            commits of the branch b after the merge base into one commit, and
                            write it as a pull request in the working tree; -n gives the name;
                            -n <pr> --update writes the pull request <pr> again and removes
                            its review; without -m, it keeps the old description
  pr ls [--json]            list the pull requests of the current checkout
  pr show <pr> [file...] [--summary] [--context N] [--lines 40-80] [-a area,...] [-x area,...]
                            print the summary and the diff with line numbers and comments;
                            --summary prints only the files, the comment counts and the
                            changes in each area; with files, print only the diff of these files
  pr comment <pr> [target] [text]
                            add a comment to review.md; with no target, on the whole pull
                            request; you commit the review when it is done
  pr apply <pr>             apply the patch to the working tree of the current branch, and do
                            not commit; use it to run the code or to continue the work; on a
                            conflict, it writes conflict markers for you to resolve
  pr apply <pr> --merge     merge: apply the patch to the working tree of the current branch,
                            remove the pull request and its todos, and stage these changes;
                            stops when the review has a comment
  pr close <pr>             remove the pull request without a merge

  The patch holds all the changes of the branch b, also in .todoer/, except the folder of the
  pull request. Run todoer done in the work: the todos that the patch removes are the todos of
  the pull request. A merge removes them by name, also when they changed on the current branch.
  A target is path:42, path:40-45, path:old:12 (a removed line), path (a file) or folder/.
  A target can name a file that the pull request does not change.
  .todoer/areas names the areas of the code, one per line: "<area>: <pattern>...".
  The patterns use the gitignore rules. The last pattern that matches a path wins.
  A pattern with no "/" matches the file name in each folder, for example "unit-tests: *.test.js".
  -a security,e2e-tests (--area) shows only the files in these areas, and -x e2e-tests
  (--exclude) hides them, in pr show.
  todoer never commits. submit, merge and close print the git command that commits their change.
  There is no approve: to accept a pull request, merge it with pr apply --merge. To ask for
  changes, commit the review and give it to an agent.

Other:
  skill [--init]            print the agent workflow, or write it as a skill; in a git repository,
                            --init writes it in the root of the repository; outside a git
                            repository, it writes it in the home folder, for all projects
  update                    install the latest release
  help, --help              print this help
  version, --version        print the version

$ todoer nope
exit code: 1
--- stderr
error: unknown command "nope" - run todoer help

$ todoer ls --nope
exit code: 1
--- stderr
error: unknown option --nope for "todoer ls" - run todoer ls --help

$ todoer pr
exit code: 1
--- stderr
error: use:
  todoer pr submit --branch <branch> -n <name> [-m <text>] [--update]
  todoer pr ls [--json]
  todoer pr show <pr> [file...] [--summary] [--context N] [--lines 40-80] [-a <area,...>] [-x <area,...>]
  todoer pr comment <pr> [target] ["<text>"]
  todoer pr apply <pr> [--merge]
  todoer pr close <pr>

$ todoer pr nope
exit code: 1
--- stderr
error: unknown command "todoer pr nope"
use:
  todoer pr submit --branch <branch> -n <name> [-m <text>] [--update]
  todoer pr ls [--json]
  todoer pr show <pr> [file...] [--summary] [--context N] [--lines 40-80] [-a <area,...>] [-x <area,...>]
  todoer pr comment <pr> [target] ["<text>"]
  todoer pr apply <pr> [--merge]
  todoer pr close <pr>

$ todoer pr show --help
use: todoer pr show <pr> [file...] [--summary] [--context N] [--lines 40-80] [-a <area,...>] [-x <area,...>]

$ todoer pr apply x --no-commit
exit code: 1
--- stderr
error: unknown option --no-commit for "todoer pr apply" - run todoer pr apply --help

$ todoer done
exit code: 1
--- stderr
error: use: todoer done <name> [<name> ...]

$ cd .. && todoer ls
exit code: 1
--- stderr
error: this folder is not in a git repository, and no folder in it has todos - run todoer in a project

